@@ -118,24 +118,36 @@
 ## The Secrets page
 
 The Secrets page lists what a machine can be granted, in three groups. The
-private parts never leave the host.
+private parts never leave the host. The header bar states the rule in one
+sentence, and the groups carry no description of their own.
 
-- The Secrets group lists the secrets of the host keyring, one row per secret. A
-  row carries the name, the mode, and the machines that hold a grant on it. The
-  page never shows a value.
+- The Tokens group lists the values of the host keyring, one row per token. A row
+  carries the name, the mode, and the machines that hold a grant on it. The page
+  never shows a value. The group carries the name Tokens, because the page
+  already carries the name Secrets.
 - A row opens the edit dialog. An empty value field keeps the stored value.
-  Add Secret stores a value in the Secret Service of the session. Delete removes
-  the value, the row, and every grant on the secret.
+  Add Token stores a value in the Secret Service of the session. Delete removes
+  the value, the row, and every grant on the token.
 - The SSH Keys group lists the keys of the session agent: the comment, the
   fingerprint, and the type. Add SSH Key loads a key file into the agent, and
   Unload drops it again. The file on disk stays.
 - The GnuPG Keys group lists the secret keys of the host agent: the uid and the
-  short fingerprint. Delete removes the secret part only, so old signatures still
-  verify.
-- Generate Key creates a key on the host: an SSH pair in ~/.ssh, or a GnuPG key
-  in the host keyring. The GnuPG agent asks for the passphrase through the
-  pinentry of the host.
-- Every row carries Grant Access, which opens the grant dialog for that entry.
+  short fingerprint. Add GnuPG Key imports a key file into the keyring, and
+  Delete removes the secret part only, so old signatures still verify.
+- Every list ends with its own actions, because a reader who adds one kind of
+  entry reads that list. Add Token sits below the tokens, and Add SSH Key and
+  Generate SSH Key sit below the SSH keys.
+- Generate SSH Key makes a key pair in ~/.ssh and loads it into the agent.
+  Generate GnuPG Key makes a key in the host keyring. The GnuPG agent asks for
+  the passphrase through the pinentry of the host.
+- A host with no token and no key still shows the three lists, so that the
+  actions of each list stay reachable.
+- Every row carries one grant control: the insert-link icon with the tooltip
+  Grant Access, which opens the grant dialog for that entry. The control is the
+  same on every row and on every kind, and it never looks like the check icon of
+  Approve or the close icon of Revoke on the Grants page.
+- The grant control and the menu of a row are icon buttons without a frame, so
+  that the suffix of a row reads as one plain strip.
 
 ## The Grants page
 
@@ -285,6 +297,19 @@ A dialog carries the Cancel control first and the affirmative control last. The
 affirmative control stays insensitive until the form is valid. The Escape key closes
 the dialog.
 
+The header bar of a dialog names the action and carries no description. Its
+subtitle states context only, such as the name of a machine. The title names the
+action without an article: Add Token, Edit Token, Generate SSH Key, Generate
+GnuPG Key, Grant Access.
+
+A dialog with a Cancel control and an affirmative control carries no close
+button, because Cancel closes it. A dialog that only reports a fact keeps the
+close button.
+
+A group of a form names its object in one word: Token, SSH Key, GnuPG Key,
+Grant. A description of a group is one sentence. A combo row keeps its choices
+short, so that the choice fits the row and the list.
+
 A dialog that carries a list carries a search button at the start of its header bar
 and a refresh control at the end. The search bar slides out below the header bar, and
 typing into the dialog opens it as well. A query without matches states itself in the
@@ -296,7 +321,8 @@ - Ctrl+N creates a machine, a secret, or a key, Ctrl+Comma opens the
   preferences, Ctrl+Question opens the shortcuts, Ctrl+W closes the window, and
   Ctrl+Q quits.
 - Ctrl+1 to Ctrl+9 select the first nine machines.
-- Every control carries a label or an accessible tooltip.
+- Every control carries a label or an accessible tooltip. A label names the
+  action and carries no ellipsis, so that a row and a dialog look the same.
 - Every list row carries a title and, where it helps, a subtitle.
 - The interface carries no color-only information. A state is always spelled out.
 - Custom styling stays at two badge classes that use the accent color variables.
@@ -314,7 +340,8 @@
 One term carries one meaning in the interface, the documentation, and the code.
 
 - A machine runs one Fedora guest. The command line calls it a virtual machine.
-- A secret is a value in the keyring of the host.
+- A token is a value in the keyring of the host. The interface writes token, the
+  code and the command line write secret.
 - A key lives in the SSH agent of the session or in the GnuPG agent of the host.
 - A grant is a time box or a single use of one secret or key for one machine.
 - A request is an agent's ask for a change or a grant.
